1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,502
Chai Corner has six people now.

2
00:00:02,802 --> 00:00:05,531
But every cup of chai
is made a different way.

3
00:00:06,081 --> 00:00:07,908
One cup takes two minutes.

4
00:00:07,908 --> 00:00:09,271
Another takes five.

5
00:00:09,571 --> 00:00:11,821
Is there one best way to make chai?

6
00:00:12,471 --> 00:00:13,862
Ravi stops guessing.

7
00:00:13,862 --> 00:00:15,254
He starts measuring.

8
00:00:15,954 --> 00:00:19,043
This is the big idea of
scientific management.

9
00:00:19,343 --> 00:00:23,098
It comes from Frederick Winslow
Taylor, an American engineer.

10
00:00:23,448 --> 00:00:26,021
He is the father of scientific management.

11
00:00:26,371 --> 00:00:31,504
In 1911, he wrote the book, The
Principles of Scientific Management.

12
00:00:31,854 --> 00:00:35,097
Study each job, and find
the one best way to do it.

13
00:00:35,747 --> 00:00:39,758
Picture a steel works in
America, around the year 1900.

14
00:00:40,108 --> 00:00:43,300
Taylor stands next to a
worker, holding a stopwatch.

15
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:47,158
Every man shovels coal and
iron ore with his own shovel.

16
00:00:47,508 --> 00:00:49,463
Taylor tests load after load.

17
00:00:49,463 --> 00:00:52,229
The best scoop is about twenty one pounds.

18
00:00:52,579 --> 00:00:56,669
So each material gets its own
shovel, sized to carry that load.

19
00:00:57,019 --> 00:01:01,246
Each man now moves about fifty
nine tons a day, up from sixteen.

20
00:01:01,896 --> 00:01:03,791
Ravi tries the same thing.

21
00:01:03,791 --> 00:01:05,952
He watches Meena make one cup.

22
00:01:06,302 --> 00:01:10,478
She boils the water, walks to fetch
the milk, and searches for the sugar.

23
00:01:10,828 --> 00:01:13,450
Ravi times every step with a stopwatch.

24
00:01:13,450 --> 00:01:15,721
This is a time and motion study.

25
00:01:16,071 --> 00:01:17,508
Then he watches Asha.

26
00:01:17,508 --> 00:01:20,194
Milk, tea and sugar are all within reach.

27
00:01:20,544 --> 00:01:21,456
No walking.

28
00:01:21,456 --> 00:01:22,468
No searching.

29
00:01:22,468 --> 00:01:25,114
Her cup takes three minutes, not five.

30
00:01:25,764 --> 00:01:29,298
Now Ravi makes Asha's way
the standard for everyone.

31
00:01:29,648 --> 00:01:30,865
One recipe card.

32
00:01:30,865 --> 00:01:33,972
The same pot, the same
cup, the same steps.

33
00:01:34,322 --> 00:01:35,907
Six people, one method.

34
00:01:35,907 --> 00:01:39,039
Every cup takes three
minutes, and tastes the same.

35
00:01:39,689 --> 00:01:42,384
Taylor built all of
this on four principles.

36
00:01:42,734 --> 00:01:44,643
Science, not rule of thumb.

37
00:01:44,643 --> 00:01:46,599
Rule of thumb means guesswork.

38
00:01:46,949 --> 00:01:48,590
Harmony, not discord.

39
00:01:48,590 --> 00:01:51,196
Managers and workers trust each other.

40
00:01:51,546 --> 00:01:53,768
Cooperation, not individualism.

41
00:01:53,768 --> 00:01:56,332
Everyone works together, not alone.

42
00:01:56,682 --> 00:02:00,241
And develop every worker, so
each person reaches their best.

43
00:02:00,891 --> 00:02:03,814
So what do his ideas
look like at work today?

44
00:02:04,164 --> 00:02:05,376
Standardisation.

45
00:02:05,376 --> 00:02:07,221
One method for everyone.

46
00:02:07,221 --> 00:02:11,141
A hospital uses the same
checklist before every surgery.

47
00:02:11,491 --> 00:02:12,947
Scientific selection.

48
00:02:12,947 --> 00:02:15,418
Pick people by testing their skills.

49
00:02:15,418 --> 00:02:18,577
A call centre tests typing
speed before hiring.

50
00:02:18,927 --> 00:02:19,827
Training.

51
00:02:19,702 --> 00:02:22,325
Teach the best method, step by step.

52
00:02:22,325 --> 00:02:25,153
New pilots practise in a flight simulator.

53
00:02:25,503 --> 00:02:26,483
Cooperation.

54
00:02:26,483 --> 00:02:28,960
Managers and workers plan together.

55
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:32,730
A factory team agrees the
shift plan before the rush.

56
00:02:33,080 --> 00:02:34,590
Functional foremanship.

57
00:02:34,590 --> 00:02:37,172
Different experts guide different things.

58
00:02:37,172 --> 00:02:41,144
One supervisor for speed, one
for quality, one for repairs.

59
00:02:41,794 --> 00:02:43,903
But was this good for the workers?

60
00:02:43,903 --> 00:02:46,057
The honest answer is, yes and no.

61
00:02:46,407 --> 00:02:47,490
The good side.

62
00:02:47,490 --> 00:02:52,215
Output went up, costs went down, and
piece rates paid faster workers more.

63
00:02:52,565 --> 00:02:53,699
The hard side.

64
00:02:53,699 --> 00:02:57,312
One small task, all day,
made work dull and tiring.

65
00:02:57,662 --> 00:02:59,573
Workers also lost their say.

66
00:02:59,573 --> 00:03:02,983
Managers did the thinking,
and workers just followed.

67
00:03:03,333 --> 00:03:08,525
In 1911, workers at the Watertown
Arsenal in America walked out when the

68
00:03:08,525 --> 00:03:09,981
stopwatch arrived.

69
00:03:10,331 --> 00:03:14,209
Congress later banned stopwatch
timing in government arsenals.

70
00:03:14,559 --> 00:03:16,185
So the lesson is balance.

71
00:03:16,185 --> 00:03:18,731
Measure the work, but respect the worker.

72
00:03:19,381 --> 00:03:20,321
Quick check.

73
00:03:20,321 --> 00:03:23,460
Every helper uses the
same pot, cup and recipe.

74
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:25,098
Which technique is this?

75
00:03:26,998 --> 00:03:28,103
Standardisation!

76
00:03:28,103 --> 00:03:31,739
The same tools and the same
method, every single time.

77
00:03:32,339 --> 00:03:33,349
So, remember.

78
00:03:33,649 --> 00:03:37,829
Scientific management means finding
the one best way, by measuring.

79
00:03:38,179 --> 00:03:42,594
Standard methods, careful selection
and training give the same result every

80
00:03:42,594 --> 00:03:43,494
time.

81
00:03:43,486 --> 00:03:45,959
But measure the work,
and respect the worker.

82
00:03:46,559 --> 00:03:51,458
Next time, we'll meet Henri Fayol, and
his rules for managing a whole company.
